From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] glm cuda: the MTP head's own passes run only without a DFlash2 drafter

Fork `a8ccc5c81` "glm cuda: the MTP head's own passes run only without a DFlash2 drafter",
with the two further head sites the fork's tip carries in cuda_prompt_pieces.run and
cuda_snapshots.planBytes.

With DFlash2 loaded the head never drafts, yet every prompt row was still absorbed into its
caches. The fork measured 61 to 86 ms of each 1.5 to 1.8 second 2,048-row chunk, 4.0 to 4.7
per cent of a cold prefill, plus the head's rows in every kept state. The drafter reads only
the head's .draft weights, so the weights stay loaded and nothing else moves. A new
Engine.head flag (drafting and no drafter) gates the absorb sites; e.mtp keeps its meaning,
which is that drafting is on and the head is loaded.

Our serve runs --drafts with --drafter, so e.mtp is true and e.head is false on it: this is
the arm where the saving lands.

The five absorb sites, all gated together so no two paths can disagree about mtp_len:
cuda_engine.prefillChunk (prompt_solo.mtpRows), cuda_prompts.pass (the batched pass),
cuda_prompt_pair.mtpRows (a paired step's two chunks), cuda_prompt_pieces.run (MULTI_FILL's
pieces) and cuda_snapshots.planBytes, which plans the head's rows for a kept state and now
plans none when the head absorbs none. keepAs and seal already take the state's own mtp_len,
which stays 0, so they need no edit.

Every other reading of e.mtp was checked and stays e.mtp, because each one asks whether the
head is loaded rather than whether it absorbs: the rank fingerprint and its drafter term,
weights.load, the shared verify buffers, Engine.draft's NoDraftHead, Lanes.facts and its
copy-confidence and NoDraftHead, cuda_snapshots.seal and keepAs, and cuda_disk_ranks.shape.
The fork's shape line is `e.mtp and !drop_mtp`, whose second term is the unported DROP_MTP
knob, so ours stays `e.mtp`.

Changed from the fork's lines: cuda_prompt_pieces.run drops the port-time comment "the pin
signals the head pass by its MTP head, not an e.head flag", which this patch makes false.
Three test engines are given the flag, because they are `undefined` apart from the fields
they set and the code now reads head: glm_snap_fixture sets `f.e.head = mtp` as the fork
does, glm_prompt_host's stand-in engine renames its `mtp` field to `head` as the fork does,
and glm_snapshot_host sets both fields at each of its two bare engines. The fork writes that
as a second statement and sets it at only one of the two, leaving its other test to read an
undefined bool; here both are set, and each is written as one destructuring assignment
because glm_snapshot_host already sits at the lean gate's 600-line limit and a second
statement would push it over. The numbers those tests assert do not move, because head true
is what they measured before.

Left out of a8ccc5c81: its cuda_prof.zig change, which adds mla_chunks and mla_merge
sections. Those belong to the latent-attention profile split that this stack has not ported,
so here they would be two enum members no code marks.

What a GPU run has to show: with --drafter loaded, the mtp profile section of a prompt chunk
goes to zero and a cold prefill loses its 4 per cent, the reply digest for a fixed prompt is
unchanged, and a turn resumed from a state kept under the new plan restores without
error.SnapshotSize. Without a drafter nothing should change at all.

Re-seated on v1.2.7 (93a2e93ecebff8aa52b88e58c5dd222516e69c5f, from pin 9c9bf542) by the 2026-11-12 re-seat (work/reseat/DECISIONS.md):
- The head absorb site in cuda_prompt_pieces.run (MULTI_FILL's pieces) is not carried here: that file belongs to the shared-round era, whose MULTI_FILL path this re-seat does not carry (DECISIONS.md, fold 0027). The four other absorb sites and planBytes are gated as in the fold.
- v1.2.7's paired step refuses a drafter (cuda_prompt_pair.pairedAt: `e.drafter != null`), so the mtpRows gate of a paired step is reached only without a drafter, where e.head equals e.mtp.
- source: work/fold/0030-head-flag.patch

